{"text": "MENTHOL\n\nTerminology - In terms of tobacco industry usage the term menthol\nis generally used in reference to the specific compound %-menthol,\nas distinguished from the isomer d-menthol. \u2018The latter plus\nother related menthols bearing prefixes such as iso, neo and\nneoiso are usually very minor constituents of commercial 1-menthol.\n\nSources - Both natural and synthetic menthol are available. The\nnatural form is obtained from the plant Mentha arvensis by\nextraction of the essential oil followed by low temperature\ncrystallization. Prior to 1939, about 70-85% of the annual world\ndemand was met by Japan. Small quantities were exported from\nChina. Because of the war and the partial disruption of trade\nroutes, cultivation of peppermint in Brazill was stimulated to\nmeet the demand in the U.S. market created by the popularity of\nmentholated cigarettes. By the end of the war Brazil had become\nthe largest producer and menthol supplier in the world market.\nBrazilian exports peaked in the early 1970's with China and\nsynthetic sources becoming available to meet the world annual\nconsumption of about 3000 tons.\n\nSynthetic menthol is produced by reacting natural occurring,\nterpines, such as pulegone, limonene, pinene, menthene, andi\nthymol with a variety of specific chemical r\u00e9agents (1,2,3).\nThymol appears to be the compound of choice for commercial\noperations. After the initial conversion menthol occurs as a\nmixture of d and \u00a2 isomers. The mixture is esterified with a\nsubstituted benzoic acid. The resulting d, t-ester can be\nselectively crystallized. The desired t-ester can be selectively\ncrystallized. The desired t-menthol is then obtained. in pure\nform by simple hydrolysis (4).\n\nThe 1980 menthol consumption was approximately 3600 tons with\n\nthe supply coming from the following sources: (5)\nNatural Sources Tons\nBrazil-Paraquay 600-700\nChina 600\nOther 325\n1595-1625\n\nSynthetic Sources\n\nGlidden 250\nHaarmann-Reimer 